\section{Translate Operands Before Looking for Redundancy}

An available-expression table keyed only by the printed operator and
join operands misses computations whose operands are defined by a merge.
Consider a pure, nontrapping binary operation at the start of a join
block, immediately after its merges. Assume each operand is either a
merge result local to the join or a value available on every incoming
edge. The lookup table is keyed by ordered SSA values and the exact
operator semantics. The proposed plan either reuses an available value
or requests insertion on that incoming edge. Assume a later stage
checks placement and implements edge splitting before rewriting the join.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Join $J$, expression $\operatorname{op}(a,b)$
\Let{P}{\varnothing}
\ForAll{incoming edges $e$ of $J$}
  \To{Select both operands using the same edge.}
  \Let{u}{\operatorname{translate}(a,J,e)}
  \Let{v}{\operatorname{translate}(b,J,e)}
  \Let{r}{\operatorname{lookupAvailable}(e,\operatorname{op},u,v)}
  \If{$r$ exists}
    \Let{P[e]}{\operatorname{reuse}(r)}
  \Else
    \To{Record a request, not a speculative edit.}
    \Let{P[e]}{\operatorname{insert}(\operatorname{op},u,v)}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return $P$
\end{sudocode}

Let the join's first operand be $p=\phi(L:a,R:b)$ and
the second $q=\phi(L:c,R:d)$. The operation is wrapping
subtraction. If $L$ has already computed \texttt{sub(a,c)},
the left translation finds that result; if $R$ has only
\texttt{sub(d,b)}, the right translation must request
\texttt{sub(b,d)}. With left values $(a,c)=(9,2)$ and right
values $(b,d)=(4,10)$, the merged result should be $7$ or
$-6$ viewed as a signed word of sufficient width. The
existing right-hand expression yields $6$ and is not
reusable. A key that sorts operands would fail this test.

On each edge, translated operands denote the values the
original expression would read after the merges, so the
replacement merge delivers exactly the original value.
This local plan does not decide whether a result is profitable
to carry across a long path, or whether the join is anticipated
from earlier blocks. Under the stated assumptions, direct
operand maps and available-expression queries take constant
time per incoming edge. If the pass is extended to expressions
later in the join block, an operand defined by an ordinary
instruction in the join cannot be translated just by consulting
the merge table.

\section{Anticipation Does Not Authorize Every Move}

The example uses modular addition so the new evaluation cannot trap, change flags visible to the source, or inspect memory. Replacing it with a checked division requires separate proof about exception timing. In particular, moving a possibly trapping operation ahead of a visible store can change whether the store takes place, even if the original division was anticipated. Loads additionally need evidence about intervening writes, and volatile accesses cannot be treated as mere reusable values. The dossier's Chapter~16, \emph{Effect-Aware IR}, motivates making these constraints visible to placement analysis. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-205} develops a representation for the memory case.

As a negative regression, split \texttt{join} into a new test followed either by the last addition or by an early return. Putting the right-arm computation before that test would now add an evaluation to the early-return path. After proving legality, a compiler can still reject a transformation because another block, a longer live range, or growth in static code outweighs the saved dynamic work.
